A bitmap that fails its checksum is not written through

Patch 0059 made the twenty-two "checksum failed" warnings audible. This
one makes seven of them act.

The seven are the bitmap sites in ext4_balloc.c and ext4_ialloc.c, and
they were not merely quiet. On a failed verify each one carried on:
modify the bitmap, recompute the checksum over the modified bytes, write
that back. A corrupt bitmap was laundered into one that verifies, and
the evidence was destroyed in the same breath -- so the next reader,
including e2fsck, found a volume that looked fine. A checksum whose
failure is invisible and whose evidence is overwritten is worse than no
checksum at all, because it is trusted.

The policy is refuse writes, allow reads, and it needs no new state
threaded through lwext4. These seven sites are reached only from
allocate and free, which are writes; the other fifteen verify structures
being read and still only report. So "refuse the write paths" is exactly
"refuse in balloc and ialloc", and a volume whose bitmaps do not verify
is still fully readable -- which is the point, because that volume may
be the only copy of something.

Refusing a free leaks: the blocks or the inode stay marked in use with
nothing pointing at them until e2fsck reclaims them. That is the
intended trade and it is not close. A leak is recoverable by a tool that
exists for it; handing the same blocks to a second file because the map
said they were free is not.

Nothing here can fire before the journal has replayed, which is where
checksum failures are expected and normal. WRITE_PROLOGUE returns EROFS
on a read-only mount and ext4b_mount recovers before anything else, so
allocate and free are unreachable until the volume is consistent.
Tests/run_bounds_tests.sh asserts that an unrecovered volume still reads.

Order mattered. Enforcement went in only after 0059 had removed the last
false positive and every suite had run silent -- turning this on over a
warning that cried wolf would have made every extent-mapped volume
read-only.

Tests/run_csum_tests.sh corrupts one byte inside the region each
checksum covers, from outside the driver, and asks both halves of the
question: was the operation refused, and did the checksum survive
untouched. Against this patch reverted, ten of its eighteen cells
fail: the write succeeds, the create succeeds, e2fsck no longer sees
anything wrong, and the checksum has been rewritten over the corruption.

